From 6db0a67923b90f15466d4e3f9641baea92b290e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] nvmem: jz4780-efuse: Bound X2600 reads to the readable area

The nvmem core bounds reads through sysfs to the device size, but not
the offsets of cells from the device tree, nor reads through
nvmem_device_read(). On the X2600 a read past the readable 120 bytes
would reach the keys, and past word 0x3f FIELD_PREP() masks the word
address, so a bad offset would silently wrap around to word 0. Refuse
reads outside the readable area before touching the controller, so that
the driver itself keeps the keys from being read.

While here, say where the read timing comes from: the RD_ADJ and
RD_STROBE split is the one of Ingenic's SPL (1 and 3 at 300 MHz), and
its total of 34 cycles is what Ingenic's other X2600 code programs too.

---
 drivers/nvmem/jz4780-efuse.c | 17 +++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/nvmem/jz4780-efuse.c b/drivers/nvmem/jz4780-efuse.c
index 34956f30d..81077c910 100644
--- a/drivers/nvmem/jz4780-efuse.c
+++ b/drivers/nvmem/jz4780-efuse.c
@@ -218,10 +218,19 @@ static int x2600_efuse_read(void *context, unsigned int offset,
 			    void *val, size_t bytes)
 {
 	struct jz4780_efuse *efuse = context;
+	const unsigned int size = X2600_EFU_READABLE * sizeof(u32);
 	u32 data[X2600_EFU_WORDS];
 	__le32 buf[X2600_EFU_WORDS];
 	int ret, i;
 
+	/*
+	 * The nvmem core bounds sysfs reads to the device size, but not the
+	 * cells' offsets or nvmem_device_read(). Past the readable words are
+	 * the keys, and past word 0x3f the address field would wrap to word 0.
+	 */
+	if (offset >= size || bytes > size - offset)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
 	mutex_lock(&efuse->lock);
 
 	/*
@@ -286,8 +295,12 @@ static int x2600_efuse_setup(struct jz4780_efuse *efuse,
 	 * In bus clock cycles:
 	 *   (rd_adj + 1) cycles > 4 ns, and
 	 *   (rd_adj + rd_strobe + 30) cycles > 100 ns.
-	 * The period is rounded down to whole nanoseconds, as in Ingenic's
-	 * code, which errs towards longer strobes: 34 cycles at 300 MHz.
+	 * The period is rounded down to whole nanoseconds, as Ingenic's
+	 * code does, which errs towards longer strobes. The split follows
+	 * Ingenic's SPL (spl_efuse.c, with the 4 ns bound): RD_ADJ 1 and
+	 * RD_STROBE 3 at 300 MHz. Its total of 34 cycles matches Ingenic's
+	 * 6.6 driver (0 and 4, from a 2 ns bound) and its U-Boot's SoC ID
+	 * check (check_socid.c, a fixed 4 and 0).
 	 * rd_strobe is signed; it is not made negative, which only makes
 	 * the strobe longer than it needs to be.
 	 */
-- 
2.43.0

